-- Attachment metadata. The bytes live on the volume, not here.
--
-- Files rather than BLOBs, for three reasons in order of how much they bite:
-- heap (SQLDelight maps BLOB to ByteArray, so every read of a 10 MB screenshot
-- is a 10 MB array through a free-trial-sized JVM, where a file streams and
-- never lands in heap); the local loop (a database carrying every screenshot
-- ever pasted is slow to copy and unpleasant to dump); and because it is the
-- same volume anyway, so the BLOB's "one thing to back up" advantage is mostly
-- notional.
--
-- The cost, honestly: writes stop being atomic. A file written whose row fails
-- to insert leaks disk; a row deleted whose file fails to unlink does the same.
-- Neither loses data. Handled the way the session sweep already is — a startup
-- pass deleting files with no matching storage_key. See AttachmentRepository.

CREATE TABLE attachments (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,

    -- Exactly one owner, enforced by the CHECK below. Five nullable columns
    -- rather than an (owner_type, owner_id) pair, because that pair cannot
    -- carry a foreign key at all — and losing FK cascade is how you end up with
    -- attachments pointing at deleted issues.
    --
    -- ── Why the forum owners are two more columns rather than a shared one ───
    --
    -- LNL-61 needed attachments in forum posts and forum comments, and the cheap
    -- move would have been to reuse `comment_id` for a forum comment. It is the
    -- wrong cheap move twice over: the column carries a foreign key at
    -- `comments(id)`, so a forum comment's id would have to *exist as an issue
    -- comment* for the row to insert at all, and the cascade would then delete
    -- the file when an unrelated issue comment went. The two id spaces are
    -- different id spaces; one column cannot name both.
    --
    -- ── The fifth column, which LNL-61 said was the deciding one ─────────────
    --
    -- That ticket left this note: *"a fifth kind of attachable thing makes it
    -- five. At that point the (owner_type, owner_id) pair starts to look better."*
    -- LNL-60 is the fifth, and the trade was re-examined rather than inherited.
    -- It still comes out this way, and the reason is the one that was already
    -- written down: the pair buys a tidier shape by giving up every cascade in
    -- this list, and there is nothing to put in its place — SQLite cannot express
    -- a foreign key whose target depends on another column's value, so the
    -- cleanup would have to move into Kotlin, into five delete paths, one of
    -- which would eventually be added without it. The failure that produces is
    -- silent: nothing breaks, the volume merely never gets smaller. Five columns
    -- and a CHECK that counts to one is ugly and cannot be got wrong.
    --
    -- What genuinely does change at five is the *arithmetic* below. Read it as
    -- "exactly one owner"; if a sixth is ever added and this is edited without
    -- the CHECK, an orphan becomes representable again.
    issue_id INTEGER,
    comment_id INTEGER,
    forum_post_id INTEGER,
    forum_comment_id INTEGER,

    -- A private message's attachment (LNL-60).
    --
    -- Worth naming what is different about this one: it is the only owner in this
    -- list that does not reach a project. Every other branch of `serveAttachment`
    -- walks up to a project and asks `canReadProject`; this one walks up to a
    -- conversation and asks whether the reader is in it. See Conversations.sq for
    -- why messages have no project, and `mayReadAttachment` for the branch.
    message_id INTEGER,

    filename TEXT NOT NULL,      -- as uploaded, for the download name
    mime_type TEXT NOT NULL,
    byte_size INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- The file's name on the volume. Random, not derived from filename: a
    -- user-supplied name reaching the filesystem is a path-traversal bug
    -- waiting for "../../lunicle.db". This column is the ONLY thing that ever
    -- names a path, and nothing a user types reaches it.
    storage_key TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,

    -- What this attachment is called in a URL, and the only part of this row a
    -- reader ever sees. Random, and that is the whole point of the column: the
    -- URL used to be `id`, so every attachment on the instance was reachable by
    -- counting, and a private project's screenshots were one guessed integer
    -- away from anybody who thought to try. See LNL-51.
    --
    -- ── Why not just use storage_key, which is already random ───────────────
    --
    -- Because it names a path. Putting it in a URL means every reader learns the
    -- filename on the volume, and it welds the public identifier to the storage
    -- layout — rename the scheme on disk and every link ever pasted dies. Two
    -- random strings is the cheaper of the two couplings.
    --
    -- ── Why existing rows get their id as their public id ───────────────────
    --
    -- Because their URLs are already written down. `/api/attachments/80` is
    -- inside the markdown of somebody's issue, in an e-mail we sent, and in
    -- links people have pasted at each other. Backfilling the decimal id makes
    -- every one of those keep working with no rewrite pass and no compatibility
    -- branch in the route — the old URL IS the new URL for those rows. They stay
    -- as guessable as they always were, which the ticket accepts explicitly;
    -- what matters is that nothing minted from here on is.
    public_id TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,

    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,
    created_by INTEGER,

    -- An author with no account, for imported history. Exclusive with
    -- created_by, same reasoning as issues — the long version lives there.
    --
    -- Here it carries a file rather than prose, which changes nothing about the
    -- attribution and everything about how the bytes arrive: an importer has no
    -- session to POST with, so uploads come through a minted ticket instead.
    -- See AttachmentTickets.
    created_by_external TEXT,

    FOREIGN KEY (issue_id) REFERENCES issues(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (comment_id) REFERENCES comments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (forum_post_id) REFERENCES forum_posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (forum_comment_id) REFERENCES forum_comments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (message_id) REFERENCES messages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (created_by) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,

    -- SQLite counts a true condition as 1, so this reads "exactly one owner is
    -- set". An orphan attachment and a doubly-owned one are both now
    -- unrepresentable rather than merely unlikely.
    CHECK (
        (issue_id IS NOT NULL) + (comment_id IS NOT NULL) +
            (forum_post_id IS NOT NULL) + (forum_comment_id IS NOT NULL) +
            (message_id IS NOT NULL) = 1
    ),

    -- One author or the other, never both. See issues.
    CHECK (created_by IS NULL OR created_by_external IS NULL)
);

insertForIssue:
INSERT INTO attachments (issue_id, filename, mime_type, byte_size, storage_key, public_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

insertForComment:
INSERT INTO attachments (comment_id, filename, mime_type, byte_size, storage_key, public_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

-- The forum's two. Separate statements rather than one with a nullable pair,
-- because the CHECK above means a statement that could set either could also set
-- both, and the failure would be a constraint violation at runtime rather than a
-- shape the caller could not express. Four narrow inserts is the whole cost of
-- four owner columns being unrepresentably-wrong rather than merely unlikely.
insertForForumPost:
INSERT INTO attachments (forum_post_id, filename, mime_type, byte_size, storage_key, public_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

insertForForumComment:
INSERT INTO attachments (forum_comment_id, filename, mime_type, byte_size, storage_key, public_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

-- ...and the private message's. Same shape, same reason.
insertForMessage:
INSERT INTO attachments (message_id, filename, mime_type, byte_size, storage_key, public_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

-- `SELECT *` rather than the columns spelled out, unlike most queries here, and
-- for a mechanical reason: it makes SQLDelight map this to the generated
-- `Attachments` type rather than minting a row class of its own, so this and
-- findByPublicId below answer with the SAME type and one mapper serves both.
-- Two spelled-out projections would be two structurally identical classes, and
-- AttachmentStore would need a copy of `toRecord` per query.
findById:
SELECT * FROM attachments WHERE id = ?;

-- The lookup the download route does, and the only one that starts from
-- something a caller supplied. Keyed on the UNIQUE public_id, so it is an index
-- seek rather than a scan, and it answers for exactly one row or none — which is
-- what lets `serveAttachment` treat an unknown id and an unreadable one as the
-- same 404.
findByPublicId:
SELECT * FROM attachments WHERE public_id = ?;

delete:
DELETE FROM attachments WHERE id = ?;

-- Every row an issue owns, directly or through one of its comments — the same
-- set `keysForIssue` names, deleted.
--
-- Redundant here and deliberately so: `ON DELETE CASCADE` already takes these
-- rows when the issue goes, and this statement removes them a moment earlier.
-- It exists because the Firestore backend has no cascade to lean on, and
-- `IssueRepository.delete` is backend-agnostic: doing it explicitly is what makes
-- the two backends agree that deleting an issue takes its files with it, the same
-- reasoning that detaches an epic's children explicitly rather than relying on
-- `ON DELETE SET NULL`. See LNL-145.
deleteForIssue:
DELETE FROM attachments
WHERE issue_id = ?
   OR comment_id IN (SELECT id FROM comments WHERE issue_id = ?);

-- Every row anywhere under a project — the same set `keysForProject` names,
-- deleted. `deleteForIssue`'s project-level twin, and redundant here for the same
-- reason: the project's cascade would take all of it anyway, a moment later. It
-- exists so `ProjectProvisioning.delete`, which is written once for both backends
-- at the seam, can drop the rows explicitly — the Firestore side has no cascade,
-- and before LNL-177 it read these keys, unlinked the files, and left every row
-- naming them behind.
deleteForProject:
DELETE FROM attachments
WHERE issue_id IN (SELECT id FROM issues WHERE project_id = ?)
   OR comment_id IN (
       SELECT comments.id FROM comments
       JOIN issues ON issues.id = comments.issue_id
       WHERE issues.project_id = ?
   )
   OR forum_post_id IN (
       SELECT forum_posts.id FROM forum_posts
       JOIN forums ON forums.id = forum_posts.forum_id
       WHERE forums.project_id = ?
   )
   OR forum_comment_id IN (
       SELECT forum_comments.id FROM forum_comments
       JOIN forum_posts ON forum_posts.id = forum_comments.post_id
       JOIN forums ON forums.id = forum_posts.forum_id
       WHERE forums.project_id = ?
   );

-- Every key the volume is allowed to hold. Read by the startup sweep, which
-- deletes any file under $VOLUME/attachments/ that is not in this set — the
-- files behind cancelled drafts and failed inserts.
allStorageKeys:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments;

-- Every attachment's declared type. Read once at startup by the link repair,
-- which has to know which ids are documents the view route will serve.
--
-- The whole table rather than a `WHERE mime_type IN (…)`: which types those are
-- is SANDBOXED_DOCUMENT_MIME_TYPES, a Kotlin set, and a copy of it spelled out in
-- SQL here would be a second list to keep in step with the first — which is the
-- exact drift the clientServer module exists to prevent. Two columns per
-- attachment, once per boot, is not a query worth being clever about.
--
-- public_id, not id: the repair rewrites URLs inside stored markdown, and a URL
-- names the public id. Selecting the row id here would have it looking for
-- `/api/attachments/41` in prose that says `/api/attachments/kQ7m…` and quietly
-- repairing nothing.
allMimeTypes:
SELECT public_id, mime_type FROM attachments;

-- Every file an issue owns, directly or through one of its comments.
--
-- Read *before* the issue is deleted, because afterwards nothing can name these
-- files: the rows go by ON DELETE CASCADE, and SQLite has no way to reach the
-- filesystem. Getting that order backwards leaves the volume holding every
-- screenshot of every deleted issue, growing forever, with nothing able to
-- identify them. See IssueRepository.delete.
keysForIssue:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments
WHERE issue_id = ?
   OR comment_id IN (SELECT id FROM comments WHERE issue_id = ?);

-- Every file one comment owns. Read before the comment is deleted, for the
-- reason above.
keysForComment:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments WHERE comment_id = ?;

-- Every file one forum post owns, directly or through one of its comments. Read
-- before the post is deleted, for the reason above. `keysForIssue`'s twin.
keysForForumPost:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments
WHERE forum_post_id = ?
   OR forum_comment_id IN (SELECT id FROM forum_comments WHERE post_id = ?);

-- Every file one forum comment owns. Same timing rule.
keysForForumComment:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments WHERE forum_comment_id = ?;

-- Every file under any post in a forum. Read before the forum is deleted — which
-- is a route a project administrator can reach, and which cascades through posts
-- and comments in one statement. Without this, deleting a forum would take every
-- row and leave every file, with nothing left able to name them.
keysForForum:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments
WHERE forum_post_id IN (SELECT id FROM forum_posts WHERE forum_id = ?)
   OR forum_comment_id IN (
       SELECT forum_comments.id FROM forum_comments
       JOIN forum_posts ON forum_posts.id = forum_comments.post_id
       WHERE forum_posts.forum_id = ?
   );

-- Every file one message owns. Same timing rule as `keysForIssue`.
keysForMessage:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments WHERE message_id = ?;

-- Every file under any message in a conversation. Read before the conversation
-- is deleted, which happens on exactly one path — discarding a conversation that
-- was started and never sent — and which cascades through its messages in one
-- statement.
keysForConversation:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments
WHERE message_id IN (SELECT id FROM messages WHERE conversation_id = ?);

-- The same, one level up: every file under any issue OR any forum post in a
-- project. Read before the project is deleted, for the reason above.
--
-- Messages are deliberately absent from this one, and their absence is not an
-- oversight of the kind the comment below describes: a conversation belongs to
-- no project (see Conversations.sq), so deleting a project cannot cascade into
-- one and there is nothing here for this query to name.
--
-- All four owners, because deleting a project cascades down both halves of it —
-- issues → comments and forums → posts → comments — and a query that named only
-- the issue side would leave every discussion attachment on the volume for ever.
-- That is the specific bug this branch of the query exists to prevent, and it is
-- silent: nothing fails, the disk merely never gets smaller.
keysForProject:
SELECT storage_key FROM attachments
WHERE issue_id IN (SELECT id FROM issues WHERE project_id = ?)
   OR comment_id IN (
       SELECT comments.id FROM comments
       JOIN issues ON issues.id = comments.issue_id
       WHERE issues.project_id = ?
   )
   OR forum_post_id IN (
       SELECT forum_posts.id FROM forum_posts
       JOIN forums ON forums.id = forum_posts.forum_id
       WHERE forums.project_id = ?
   )
   OR forum_comment_id IN (
       SELECT forum_comments.id FROM forum_comments
       JOIN forum_posts ON forum_posts.id = forum_comments.post_id
       JOIN forums ON forums.id = forum_posts.forum_id
       WHERE forums.project_id = ?
   );
